-- allium: 3
-- sync.allium
--
-- Scope: This board's relationship with the shared store — coming up, staying
--        up, coming back, and what it says while it is down. Also what a board
--        asks the store to send it, the far more important question of what it
--        does not, where the rows it draws come from, and where the changes it
--        makes go.
-- Includes: the connection's lifecycle and the retry that drives it; the
--           identity handshake that must complete before anything syncs;
--           subscribing to and unsubscribing from an epic; the rule that stops
--           a board whose identity no longer matches; what an operator sees
--           while the store is unreachable; WHERE THE BOARD READS ITS ROWS
--           FROM, and what it draws when the answer is unreachable; WHERE A
--           CHANGE GOES, what happens to one made while the store is down, and
--           what the store does with one it will not accept.
-- Excludes: which store, and how it is spoken to — nothing here names a
--           protocol, a socket or a vendor, and a correct implementation over
--           any transport satisfies this file. The shape of what travels (see
--           core.allium: Task, Epic, Host, Subscription). The snapshot escape
--           hatch and the id-sequence obligation (see spacetime-seed.allium).
--           Whose identity this install holds and how it got it (see
--           host.allium: AdoptUserIdentity and the two rules beside it) — this
--           file offers the identity and consumes the verdict, and deliberately
--           does not decide it. WHICH rows a card is DRAWN from once they are
--           here (see board-layout.allium, board-visuals.allium) — this file
--           says where they came from and says nothing about their arrangement.
--           WHICH mutations exist and what each one means (see tasks.allium,
--           epics.allium, todo.allium) — this file says only where a mutation
--           GOES and what happens when it cannot get there. Anything that
--           spawns a process: dispatching an agent, provisioning or releasing
--           a worktree, running a feed's command. Those are jobs for the
--           machine the operator is sitting at and no store can do them, so
--           they stay local whatever this file says about rows.
--
-- Why this exists as its own file. host.allium answers "who am I?" and
-- core.allium answers "what is a task?"; neither is the right home for a thing
-- that is up or down. A connection has a lifecycle, a failure mode that repeats,
-- and a visible state — which is to say it is a domain entity, and it did not
-- have one before.
--
-- THE HONEST SHAPE OF THE PROBLEM. A shared store reachable over a network is
-- down sometimes. Not as an exception: as a matter of course, for a few seconds,
-- several times a day, whenever a laptop lid closes or a VPN reconnects. A
-- design that treats disconnection as an error to report once has specified the
-- rare case and left the common one to whatever the code happens to do. So the
-- states below are not "working" and "broken". They are the four states a
-- connection is actually ever in, and three of them are ordinary.
--
-- ONE OF THEM IS NOT ORDINARY, and it is the only fatal thing in this file: the
-- store answering with a different person than the one this install has stored.
-- See StopOnAUserIdentityConflict, and host.allium: RefuseAChangedUserIdentity
-- for why that is worth stopping a board over when nothing else here is.
--
-- A BOARD WITH NO STORE CONFIGURED IS NOT A DEGRADED BOARD. No BoardConnection
-- exists, no rule below fires, nothing is reported and nothing is missing. It
-- reads its rows from the local store, as it always has. That is the
-- single-machine install dispatch has always been, and it stays a first-class
-- way to run rather than an unconfigured state to nag about.
--
-- THE ONE PLACE A BOARD IS REALLY WORSE OFF THAN BEFORE. A board that IS
-- configured reads only the store, and a store that is down means a board with
-- no cards on it. There is no fallback to the local copy and there must not be
-- one: core.allium's shared tables have exactly one copy, and a board that
-- silently served an older one would be showing work whose current state it
-- cannot see — a colleague's task that has since been finished, reassigned or
-- deleted, drawn as though it were live. An empty board with an outage on it is
-- a worse experience and a truer one. See BoardReadsFromTheSubscription and
-- ADisconnectedBoardDrawsNoSharedRows below.
--
-- AND IT CANNOT WRITE EITHER. The same bargain, on the other side: a board
-- whose store is down refuses the mutation, says so, and changes nothing
-- anywhere. It does not queue it for later and it does not fall back to the
-- local copy. A queue would be a second authority over a shared row — replayed
-- minutes later, on top of whatever a colleague did meanwhile, with the
-- operator's original intent long since acted on elsewhere. See
-- BoardWritesThroughTheStore and NoWriteIsEverQueued.

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- This board's one connection to the shared store.
--
-- It exists only where a store is configured. Absence is not a state below and
-- is not a failure: it is the local-only install, and every rule in this file
-- is silent for it.
entity BoardConnection {
    -- Where the shared store is. A field rather than config because it is per
    -- install, not per product: two people share a store by both naming it, and
    -- a default would silently point a board at somewhere nobody chose.
    server: String

    status: connecting | connected | disconnected | failed

    -- Consecutive failed attempts since the last success. Reset on connect,
    -- not decayed: this counts the current outage, and an outage that ends is
    -- over rather than partly over. Its only consumer is the backoff.
    attempts: Integer

    -- What went wrong, in terms an operator can act on. Present exactly while
    -- something is wrong, which is what makes the pair of states below
    -- self-describing rather than needing a reader to remember which one
    -- carries a reason.
    last_error: String when status = disconnected | failed

    -- When the outage began, in the sense the backoff cares about: the moment
    -- of the most recent failure, not of the first. Present only while
    -- retrying, and cleared on the way out, so it cannot be read as the age of
    -- an outage that has ended.
    last_failure_at: Timestamp when status = disconnected

    -- When RetryAfterBackoff below fires. Exponential in the number of
    -- consecutive failures, from config.reconnect_backoff_base, and never
    -- beyond config.reconnect_backoff_max past the last failure.
    --
    -- A derived instant rather than a stored delay, because a delay has to be
    -- counted down by something and an instant is simply true or not yet. It
    -- also makes the retry a temporal trigger like every other scheduled thing
    -- in this system, rather than a timer the board has to remember to keep.
    next_attempt_at: last_failure_at + min(
        config.reconnect_backoff_base * pow(2, attempts - 1),
        config.reconnect_backoff_max
    )

    -- The epics this board has asked for. Derived from the shared Subscription
    -- rows rather than held here, so that subscribing on the desktop is already
    -- true on the laptop — the subscription belongs to the PERSON, and the
    -- connection is a machine's.
    subscribed_epics: core/Subscriptions.where(s => s.subscriber = core/local_host().owner) -> epic

    -- WHAT A CONNECTION OWNS BESIDES ITS STATUS: the rows its subscriptions
    -- have delivered.
    --
    -- Not a field, because the rows are of every shared entity at once and this
    -- language has no union to type that with. It is stated here rather than
    -- left implicit because the OWNERSHIP is the load-bearing part:
    -- ADisconnectedBoardDrawsNoSharedRows is a consequence of the rows
    -- belonging to the connection, not an extra rule somebody has to remember.
    --
    -- A set that outlived its connection would be a cache, and a cache is
    -- precisely what must not exist here: core.allium's shared tables have one
    -- copy, and a second one served during an outage would draw a colleague's
    -- finished work as though it were live. See BoardReadsFromTheSubscription.
    --
    -- The implementation's counterpart is `SharedRows` (`src/sync/rows.rs`),
    -- held beside the transport. Where it sits does not matter; that it is
    -- emptied with the connection does.

    @invariant AConnectedBoardDrawsOnlyDeliveredRows
        -- On a board with a connection, every shared row on screen arrived
        -- through that connection's subscriptions. There is no second reader.
        --
        -- The counterpart to SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics,
        -- which constrains the ASK. This constrains the READ, and the pair is
        -- what makes the containment claim complete: asking only for your own
        -- rows protects nobody if the reader can also reach the local store's
        -- copy of everyone else's.
        --
        -- Not stated as an expression because the two sides — what was
        -- delivered and what is drawn — are sets of every shared entity at
        -- once, which this language has no union to name. What is checkable is
        -- a property of the CODE rather than of a state: exactly one read path
        -- exists (`crate::sync::BoardReads`), and the implementation the
        -- runtime picks when a store is configured holds no handle to the local
        -- store to reach through. `sync::tests::board_reads` asserts it
        -- directly, by standing a fully populated local database beside an
        -- empty subscription and requiring every read to answer empty.
        --
        -- An install with no connection is out of scope: it reads locally and
        -- always has, and every row there is its own.

    -- The identity handshake has completed and this board may sync.
    --
    -- Written as a derivation over the host row rather than as a field here
    -- because there is exactly one stored identity and it lives on the host
    -- (core/Host.owner). A second copy on the connection would be a second
    -- thing to clear on disconnect, and forgetting to clear it is how a board
    -- keeps syncing as somebody it no longer is.
    is_identified: core/local_host().owner != null

    transitions status {
        connecting -> connected
        connecting -> disconnected
        connected -> disconnected
        connected -> failed
        disconnected -> connecting
        terminal: failed

        -- THERE IS NO `connecting -> failed` EDGE, and its absence is a claim
        -- rather than an omission. The only thing that reaches `failed` is an
        -- identity conflict, and a conflict is discovered downstream of
        -- acceptance — a store that has not accepted the connection has
        -- offered no identity for anyone to disagree with. An edge here would
        -- be one no rule witnesses, which is to say a state a reader could
        -- reach in their head and the system never in fact enters.
    }

    -- One board, one connection. Two would mean two subscription sets and two
    -- retry clocks against one store, and the second one would be invisible:
    -- nothing an operator sees distinguishes one healthy connection from one
    -- healthy connection beside a thrashing one.
    invariant AtMostOneBoardConnection {
        BoardConnections.count <= 1
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The wait before the first retry after a failed attempt. Doubles per
    -- consecutive attempt up to the ceiling below.
    --
    -- Short on purpose. The overwhelmingly common outage is a few seconds long
    -- — a lid, a tunnel, a wifi handover — and a board that waits half a minute
    -- to notice the network came back is a board that feels broken for half a
    -- minute after it is fixed.
    reconnect_backoff_base: Duration = 1.second

    -- The longest the board will ever wait between attempts.
    --
    -- The ceiling exists for the other outage: the store is down for an hour,
    -- and an unbounded backoff would have the board checking once a day by the
    -- time somebody fixes it. A minute is short enough that a person who
    -- repaired the store sees the board recover while still looking at it.
    reconnect_backoff_max: Duration = 60.seconds

    -- How long one attempt may hang before it counts as failed.
    --
    -- Needed because the interesting failure is not refusal, it is silence: a
    -- host that accepts a connection and never answers leaves a board in
    -- `connecting` forever, which reports as "starting up" rather than as the
    -- outage it is. Without this the state machine has no edge out of that.
    connect_timeout: Duration = 10.seconds

    -- How long one mutation may wait for the store's answer.
    --
    -- The same argument as connect_timeout, one layer in. A store that accepts
    -- a change and never answers would otherwise leave the caller waiting
    -- forever, and nothing else would break the wait: a drop is noticed only if
    -- the transport closes, and a store that has stopped answering need not
    -- have.
    --
    -- Shorter than connect_timeout deliberately. A connection is set up once
    -- and may cross a slow link; a mutation happens while somebody is looking
    -- at the screen.
    --
    -- EXPIRY IS NOT A REFUSAL. The change was sent, so it may have been
    -- applied — see the open question about a write whose fate is unknown.
    mutation_timeout: Duration = 5.seconds
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Coming up ==

rule OpenBoardConnection {
    when: BoardDrawn()

    requires:
        configured_store_server() != null
        and BoardConnections.count = 0

    ensures:
        BoardConnection.created(
            server: configured_store_server(),
            status: connecting,
            attempts: 0
        )

    @guidance
        -- THE BOARD IS ALREADY DRAWN WHEN THIS FIRES, and that ordering is the
        -- single most load-bearing decision in the file. It is why the trigger
        -- is startup.allium's BoardDrawn and not something earlier in that
        -- pipeline: a start that waited on a round trip would be as slow as the
        -- slowest network the board has ever been on, and a store that is merely
        -- SLOW would be indistinguishable from a board that has hung.
        --
        -- So a board draws BEFORE it is connected, spends its first moments in
        -- `connecting`, and is fully useful there. What it shows meanwhile is
        -- the local store, exactly as it always has — see this file's Excludes.
        -- The cost of a cold start is therefore not a round trip; it is nothing,
        -- and the Open Questions below say when that stops being true.
        --
        -- NO STORE CONFIGURED, NO CONNECTION. The guard is the whole of that:
        -- the rule does not fire, no entity is created, and nothing downstream
        -- has an opinion about it. A single-machine install is not an
        -- unconfigured one.
        --
        -- The count guard makes a second board start in the same process a
        -- no-op rather than a second connection — see AtMostOneBoardConnection
        -- for why two would be worse than visibly broken.
}

rule ConnectionAccepted {
    when: ConnectionAccepted(connection, identity)

    requires: connection.status = connecting

    ensures:
        connection.status = connected
        connection.attempts = 0
        UserIdentityOffered(identity: identity)

    @guidance
        -- The store accepted the connection and told us who it thinks we are.
        -- That answer is not evaluated here: it goes to host.allium, which owns
        -- both the stored identity and the decision about a changed one, and
        -- comes back as UserIdentitySettled or UserIdentityConflict.
        --
        -- The split is deliberate and it is not ceremony. "Is this the same
        -- person?" is a question about identity, and answering it beside the
        -- socket is how a retry loop ends up with an opinion about who you are.
        --
        -- `attempts` resets here rather than on the next failure, so the backoff
        -- of a NEW outage starts short. A counter that survived a successful
        -- connection would punish a board for an outage that is over.
        --
        -- The identity ARRIVES WITH THE ACCEPTANCE rather than being fetched
        -- afterwards. A store that accepted a connection without saying who it
        -- thinks you are would leave a window in which the board is up and
        -- nobody — and every rule downstream would need an arm for it.
}

rule SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled {
    when: UserIdentitySettled(identity)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        SubscriptionsAsserted(
            connection: connection,
            owner_board: identity,
            epics: connection.subscribed_epics
        )

    @guidance
        -- NOTHING SYNCS BEFORE THE IDENTITY IS SETTLED, and the ordering is the
        -- point of having a separate rule. A board that subscribed on connect
        -- and settled its identity afterwards would, in the conflict case,
        -- already have asked for a stranger's rows before discovering it was a
        -- stranger.
        --
        -- WHAT IS ASKED FOR IS EXACTLY TWO THINGS: this person's own user board,
        -- and the epics they subscribe to. The own board is not a Subscription
        -- row and never will be — see core/Subscription's commentary — it is
        -- implied by the identity, which is also why there is no way to express
        -- an interest in somebody else's. That is not a permission check that
        -- could be got wrong; it is an operation that does not exist. See
        -- SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics.
        --
        -- RE-ASSERTED ON EVERY CONNECTION, unconditionally, including the
        -- reconnects where nothing changed. Subscriptions are per-connection
        -- and a dropped connection takes them with it; re-subscribing is
        -- idempotent by core/Subscription's uniqueness invariant, so the cost of
        -- asserting an unchanged set is a round trip and the cost of assuming it
        -- survived is a board that silently stops updating.
}

-- == What the board draws ==

rule BoardReadsFromTheSubscription {
    when: BoardNeedsRows()

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection != null

    ensures:
        RowsDrawnFromTheSubscription(connection)

    @guidance
        -- ONE SOURCE, CHOSEN BY WHETHER A STORE IS CONFIGURED. The guard is
        -- `connection != null`, which is the same condition
        -- OpenBoardConnection fires on, so the two cannot disagree: a board
        -- that has a connection draws from it, and a board that has none draws
        -- from the local store as it always has.
        --
        -- NOT "the subscription, falling back to local". There is no third
        -- branch and adding one would undo the design: a shared table has
        -- exactly one copy, so the local rows on a configured board's disk are
        -- not a cache of the store — they are whatever that install last wrote
        -- before it started sharing, which may be months stale and may name
        -- tasks that no longer exist. Serving them during an outage would put
        -- a colleague's finished work back on screen as though it were live.
        --
        -- WHAT ARRIVES IS WHAT WAS ASKED FOR, and nothing else. The reader has
        -- no path past the subscription, which is what makes
        -- SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics worth anything: an ask
        -- constrained to your own rows means nothing if the reader can reach
        -- around it.
        --
        -- A ROW THAT CANNOT BE UNDERSTOOD IS DROPPED, not defaulted and not
        -- fatal. A task carrying a status this binary does not know is missing
        -- from the board rather than sitting on it under a plausible wrong one,
        -- and the rows beside it are unaffected. The same bargain the local
        -- store's bulk reads already make.
}

rule SubscribedRowsArriveUnasked {
    when: StoreDeliversRow(connection, row)

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        SubscribedRowAccepted(connection, row)
        and BoardRedrawn()

    @guidance
        -- THIS IS WHAT REPLACES POLLING, and it is the visible difference a
        -- shared board makes. A colleague moves a card on their machine and it
        -- moves here — not within a refresh interval, but as the row arrives.
        --
        -- The redraw reads the WHOLE board rather than applying the row as a
        -- delta. The delta is the store's bookkeeping and reconstructing it
        -- here would be a second copy of it, free to drift; re-reading what is
        -- already in memory costs nothing worth saving it over.
        --
        -- A periodic refresh MAY still exist as a backstop and is not a
        -- contradiction: it changes nothing when nothing has changed. What
        -- would be a contradiction is a board that depends on it, because that
        -- board is one whose colleague's work is always up to one interval old.
}

rule ADisconnectedBoardDrawsNoSharedRows {
    when: ConnectionDropped(connection, reason)

    ensures:
        SubscribedRowsDiscarded(connection)

    @guidance
        -- The rows belonged to that connection. Subscriptions are
        -- per-connection (SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled says so), the next one
        -- re-delivers from scratch, and rows kept across the gap would be a
        -- board's contents on screen with nothing live behind them — arrived at
        -- by accident rather than chosen, which is the worst way to have a
        -- cache.
        --
        -- SO A DISCONNECTED BOARD IS AN EMPTY BOARD, and that is accepted
        -- rather than regretted. It is also why ConnectionIndicator's
        -- DisconnectedIsAlwaysVisible is not a nicety: without it, "the store
        -- is down" and "you have nothing to do" look identical.
        --
        -- The reconnect is ordinary and fast (config.reconnect_backoff_base is
        -- a second), so the state this describes is normally a blink. It is
        -- specified because the outage that is NOT a blink is the one somebody
        -- has to understand.
}

-- == What the board writes ==

rule BoardWritesThroughTheStore {
    when: SharedRowMutationRequested(mutation)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires:
        connection != null
        and connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        MutationSentToStore(connection, mutation)

    @guidance
        -- THE MIRROR OF BoardReadsFromTheSubscription, and it has to be, or the
        -- two halves disagree about which copy is real. A board that read the
        -- store and wrote its own disk would make every change it performed
        -- invisible to itself the moment it was made: written to a copy nothing
        -- draws from, absent from the copy everything draws from, and reported
        -- as a success.
        --
        -- The guard is the same `connection != null` both other rules use, so a
        -- board with no store configured writes locally as it always has. That
        -- is the single-machine install and not a branch to be ashamed of.
        --
        -- NOT EVERY MUTATION IN THE SYSTEM — every mutation of a SHARED row.
        -- This person's preferences, the knowledge base and its embeddings, and
        -- usage telemetry are this machine's and are written here regardless.
        -- The line is core.allium's, not this file's.
        --
        -- AND NOT ANYTHING THAT RUNS A PROCESS. Dispatching an agent,
        -- provisioning a worktree, releasing one, running a feed's command:
        -- those happen on the machine the operator is sitting at, because a
        -- store has no terminal, no checkout and no git. What reaches the store
        -- is the ROW that records the outcome, and the order matters — the
        -- worktree exists before the row that names it does.
}

rule RefuseToStartWhereOnlySomeChangesReachTheStore {
    when: BoardDrawn()

    requires:
        configured_store_server() != null
        and not every_shared_change_reaches_the_store()

    ensures:
        StartupAborted(reason: "some changes would not reach the store")
        and BoardConnections.count = 0

    @guidance
        -- THE ONE STATE WORSE THAN NOT HAVING THE FEATURE, and the reason it is
        -- worth a rule of its own rather than a note in a plan.
        --
        -- BoardWritesThroughTheStore says every shared change goes to the
        -- store. While that is only MOSTLY true — while the migration that
        -- moves them is part way through — a configured board writes some
        -- tables to the store and some to its own disk. Nothing looks wrong.
        -- Creating a task reaches the store; starting an agent on it does not;
        -- the two disagree from that moment, and the first person to notice is
        -- a colleague looking at a task with no session on it.
        --
        -- REFUSING IS THE SMALLER HARM. The operator finds out at the moment
        -- they point the board at a store, which is the moment they can still
        -- change their mind, rather than a week later from somebody else's
        -- screen.
        --
        -- It aborts rather than degrading to a local board, and the difference
        -- matters: an operator who asked for a shared board and silently got a
        -- private one would go on believing their work was shared. Same
        -- reasoning as startup.allium's TheBoardNeverDrawsForAnUnnamedHost.
        --
        -- This rule is expected to stop firing. `every_shared_change_reaches_the_store`
        -- is a property of the implementation rather than of any board's state,
        -- and the phase that finishes the move makes it true for good. It stays
        -- in the spec afterwards because the next migration of this shape wants
        -- the same guard, and because a rule that has been deleted cannot
        -- explain why the guard existed.
}

rule AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused {
    when: SharedRowMutationRequested(mutation)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires:
        connection != null
        and connection.status != connected

    ensures:
        MutationRefused(connection, mutation, reason: connection.last_error)
        and not MutationSentToStore(connection, mutation)

    @guidance
        -- IT FAILS, LOUDLY, AND NOTHING ANYWHERE CHANGES. Not queued, not
        -- retried, not written to the local copy as a stand-in. The caller
        -- learns it did not happen, at the moment it did not happen, and the
        -- operator sees the refusal rather than discovering it later.
        --
        -- WHY NOT A QUEUE, stated once so nobody has to relitigate it. A queued
        -- write is an intent formed against a board state that no longer holds
        -- by the time it lands. The operator moved a card to done because of
        -- what they could see; three minutes later a colleague has reassigned
        -- that task, and replaying the move writes a decision nobody would make
        -- now. Worse, the operator was told it worked and has moved on. A
        -- refusal is a smaller harm and an honest one: the person is still
        -- looking at the screen and can decide for themselves.
        --
        -- THE REFUSAL IS NOT THE OUTAGE REPORT. ConnectionIndicator already
        -- says the store is down, continuously; this says that THIS action did
        -- not happen. Both are needed — an indicator alone leaves a person
        -- guessing whether the thing they just pressed went through.
        --
        -- The reason is the connection's, because that is the true one. "The
        -- store is unreachable: connection refused" is actionable; "could not
        -- save" is not.
}

rule StoreRejectsAnInvalidMutation {
    when: StoreRejectedMutation(connection, mutation, reason)

    ensures:
        MutationRefused(connection, mutation, reason: reason)

    @guidance
        -- VALIDATION HAPPENS WHERE THE ROWS ARE. A rule about a shared row is a
        -- rule about the one copy of it, and a board can only ever check it
        -- against the subset it subscribes to — so a check performed here is a
        -- check performed against partial knowledge, and two boards holding
        -- different subsets would reach different verdicts about the same
        -- write. The store is the only party that sees all of it.
        --
        -- REJECTION IS ALL-OR-NOTHING. A mutation the store refuses leaves it
        -- exactly as it was, including any derived value the mutation would
        -- have triggered. A half-applied write is the one outcome no caller
        -- can recover from, because there is no way to ask what part landed.
        --
        -- The reason travels back and is shown. A store that refused writes
        -- without saying why would turn every validation rule into a board that
        -- mysteriously does nothing.
        --
        -- The board does NOT pre-check to save a round trip. A duplicated
        -- validator is a validator free to disagree with the real one, and the
        -- disagreement surfaces as either a refusal the store would have
        -- allowed or, far worse, a confident local "yes" the store then refuses
        -- anyway. One authority.
}

-- == Staying up, and coming back ==

rule ConnectionAttemptFailed {
    when: ConnectionAttemptFailed(connection, reason)

    requires: connection.status = connecting

    ensures:
        connection.status = disconnected
        connection.attempts = connection.attempts + 1
        connection.last_error = reason
        connection.last_failure_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Covers refusal, an unresolvable host, a rejected credential and the
        -- attempt that simply never answered within config.connect_timeout.
        -- They are one state because an operator's next action is the same for
        -- all of them — look at the reason — and the reason is carried, so
        -- nothing is lost by not branching.
        --
        -- THIS IS THE FIRST-START FAILURE TOO. A board whose store was never
        -- reachable lands here, on attempt 1, with the reason set, and that is
        -- what "startup surfaces a clear error" means: the board is up, it is
        -- disconnected, and the indicator says why. It is not an abort. The
        -- board's own data is local and a store that is down does not make it
        -- unreadable.
        --
        -- The counter increments here and nowhere else, which keeps the backoff
        -- reading one number that means one thing.
}

rule ConnectionDropped {
    when: ConnectionDropped(connection, reason)

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        connection.status = disconnected
        connection.attempts = connection.attempts + 1
        connection.last_error = reason
        connection.last_failure_at = now

    @guidance
        -- A connection that WAS up and is no longer. Separate from
        -- ConnectionAttemptFailed because the two are reached from different
        -- states and the transition graph says so, but they land in the same
        -- place on purpose: from here on, recovery is identical, and a design
        -- with two flavours of disconnected would have two retry loops to keep
        -- in step.
        --
        -- THERE IS NO SUCH THING AS AN UNNOTICED DROP. A store that stops
        -- answering without closing anything is the normal case on a sleeping
        -- laptop, so a connection that only learns of drops from the far end is
        -- one that reports `connected` at a screen nobody is updating. What
        -- makes this trigger fire in that case is not specified here — it is a
        -- liveness question, not a domain one — but that it MUST fire is.
        --
        -- `attempts` increments here too, so an outage that begins from a
        -- healthy connection backs off exactly like one that begins from a
        -- failed attempt.
}

rule RetryAfterBackoff {
    when: connection: BoardConnection.next_attempt_at <= now

    requires: connection.status = disconnected

    ensures:
        connection.status = connecting
        connection.last_error = null
        connection.last_failure_at = null

    @guidance
        -- The whole of the reconnect loop, and the reason this file exists at
        -- all: nothing under the board does this for us. A client library that
        -- reconnects on its own would make this rule redundant; one that does
        -- not makes it the difference between a board that recovers and a board
        -- that needs restarting after every wifi handover.
        --
        -- The delay is exponential from config.reconnect_backoff_base, doubling
        -- per consecutive attempt, capped at config.reconnect_backoff_max — see
        -- both for why each end of that range is where it is.
        --
        -- The `requires` is what stops it re-firing: the first thing the rule
        -- does is leave `disconnected`, so the temporal condition cannot hold
        -- again until a later failure puts it back.
        --
        -- IT DOES NOT FIRE FROM `failed`. That state is terminal in the graph
        -- above, and deliberately: retrying an identity conflict retries it
        -- forever, since nothing about a retry changes which person the store
        -- believes this is.
}

-- == Stopping ==

rule StopOnAUserIdentityConflict {
    when: UserIdentityConflict(stored, offered)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        connection.status = failed
        connection.last_error = identity_conflict_message(stored, offered)

    @guidance
        -- The one fatal condition in this file. host.allium:
        -- RefuseAChangedUserIdentity has already refused the write and explained
        -- why; this is what refusing costs the board.
        --
        -- `failed` is terminal, so no retry follows and no subscription is
        -- asserted. A board in this state syncs nothing. Whether it also stops
        -- being a board at all is the difference between a cold start and a
        -- mid-session drop, and it is the same answer for both: the board does
        -- not continue to run as somebody. It reports the conflict, names both
        -- identities, and stops.
        --
        -- ONE STATE FOR BOTH ARRIVAL PATHS is what keeps that answer honest. The
        -- tempting design has a fatal cold start and a warning mid-session,
        -- because stopping a board somebody is working in feels rude. It is
        -- less rude than the alternative, which is continuing to accept work
        -- under an identity that owns none of it.
        --
        -- The `connected` guard is not a narrowing, it is the only state this
        -- can be reached from. The conflict is decided downstream of
        -- ConnectionEstablished, which fires on the edge INTO `connected`, so a
        -- connection that is not up has offered no identity for anyone to
        -- disagree with. Written out rather than left implicit because it is
        -- what makes the `failed` edge one the transition graph above admits.
}

-- == Choosing what to follow ==

rule SubscribeToEpic {
    when: SubscribeToEpic(operator, epic)

    requires: core/local_host().owner != null

    ensures: core/Subscription.created(
        subscriber: core/local_host().owner,
        epic: epic
    )

    @guidance
        -- Following an epic means its tasks reach this person's board, on every
        -- machine they use, until they say otherwise. The row is the person's,
        -- not the machine's — which is why it is a shared Subscription rather
        -- than a local preference, and why subscribing on the laptop is already
        -- true on the desktop.
        --
        -- REQUIRES AN IDENTITY, because a subscription is one person's standing
        -- interest and an install that has never connected does not know whose.
        -- This is the one place the null owner from core/Host bites, and it
        -- bites correctly: there is nothing to subscribe to a store this board
        -- has never reached.
        --
        -- IDEMPOTENT, by core/Subscription's SubscriptionIsUniquePerSubscriberAndEpic.
        -- Subscribing twice is subscribing; there is no error to report and
        -- nothing for a caller to check first.
        --
        -- THERE IS NO SubscribeToUserBoard, HERE OR ANYWHERE. Not refused — 
        -- absent. Your own board needs no row (the identity implies it) and
        -- somebody else's is theirs. An operation that could be called and
        -- refused would be an operation whose refusal could be got wrong.
}

rule UnsubscribeFromEpic {
    when: UnsubscribeFromEpic(operator, epic)

    let subscription = core/Subscription{subscriber: core/local_host().owner, epic: epic}

    requires: subscription != null

    ensures: not exists subscription

    @guidance
        -- The epic's tasks stop reaching this board. Reversible at any time by
        -- subscribing again, and carrying nothing with it: a subscription is an
        -- interest, not an assignment, so dropping one changes no task, no
        -- owner and no worktree.
        --
        -- IT DOES NOT AND CANNOT UNSUBSCRIBE ANYBODY ELSE. The row is looked up
        -- by this install's own identity, so there is no argument through which
        -- a caller could name another subscriber.
        --
        -- The `requires` makes unsubscribing from something unfollowed a
        -- refusal rather than a silent no-op. The asymmetry with SubscribeToEpic
        -- above, which is happily idempotent, is deliberate: subscribing twice
        -- expresses what the caller wanted, and unsubscribing from nothing means
        -- the caller was wrong about the state they were in.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The containment claim of the whole design, stated where it can be read.
--
-- A board asks for its own user board and its subscribed epics. The
-- interesting half is the converse: nothing else was asked for, so a store
-- that honours the request sends nothing else, and a colleague's private work
-- is not on this machine to be leaked, searched or accidentally rendered.
--
-- Written against the Subscription rows rather than against arriving data
-- because that is the half this system controls. What a store chooses to send
-- an under-subscribed client is the store's to get right; what this board asks
-- for is this file's, and asking for too much is the failure that would be
-- ours.
invariant SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics {
    for s in core/Subscriptions:
        s.subscriber = core/local_host().owner implies s.epic != null
}

-- NoWriteIsEverQueued:
-- No write waits anywhere for a store to come back.
--
-- The property behind AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused, stated as a fact about
-- the state so that it survives somebody adding a retry to a different layer
-- later. There is no buffer, no outbox, no pending set — not an empty one, none
-- at all. An empty queue is a queue that a well-meaning change can start
-- filling; an absent one has to be designed back in on purpose.
--
-- Prose rather than an expression on purpose. An expression would have to name
-- the collection it claims is empty, and naming it is the first half of adding
-- it. There is nothing here to quantify over, which is exactly the claim.

-- A connection that has not settled an identity has not subscribed.
--
-- The ordering guarantee of SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled, restated as a
-- property of the state so it survives somebody adding a second path to
-- subscribing later.
invariant NothingIsSubscribedBeforeAnIdentityExists {
    for c in BoardConnections:
        c.subscribed_epics.count > 0 implies c.is_identified
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Connecting, subscribing and reading the indicator are all operator actions.
-- There is deliberately no agent-facing route to any of them: a dispatched
-- agent works inside one task, and changing which epics its operator follows —
-- or taking the board's connection down — is not within that.
actor Operator {
    identified_by: core/Host where id != ""
}

-- The far side of the connection. An actor rather than an unnamed outside,
-- because the three events it delivers are a contract this board depends on
-- and a store that delivers only two of them leaves the board in a state with
-- no way out — see StoreAnnouncesEveryTransition on the surface below.
actor SharedStore {
    identified_by: BoardConnection where server != ""
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface StoreBoundary {
    facing _: SharedStore

    context connection: BoardConnection

    exposes:
        connection.server
        connection.subscribed_epics

    provides:
        ConnectionAccepted(connection, identity)
            when connection.status = connecting
        ConnectionAttemptFailed(connection, reason)
            when connection.status = connecting
        ConnectionDropped(connection, reason)
            when connection.status = connected
        StoreDeliversRow(connection, row)
            when connection.status = connected
        StoreRejectedMutation(connection, mutation, reason)
            when connection.status = connected

    @guarantee StoreAnnouncesEveryTransition
        -- Every attempt ends in exactly one of acceptance or failure, and every
        -- accepted connection ends in a drop. A store that lets an attempt hang
        -- without answering, or stops serving without closing, strands the
        -- board in `connecting` or in a `connected` state that is a lie.
        -- config.connect_timeout exists because the first of those cannot be
        -- left to the far end's good behaviour.
        --
        -- THE DROP IS THE TRANSPORT'S TO NOTICE, not the board's to discover.
        -- Nothing above the transport can see a socket die: the board would
        -- find out by failing to do something, and a connected board that is
        -- doing nothing never fails at anything. So a drop is reported, not
        -- polled for — and a transport that cannot report one leaves every rule
        -- below it with no way out of `connected`.
        --
        -- REPORTED ONCE. The same drop announced twice would re-enter
        -- `disconnected` and restart the backoff, turning a long outage into a
        -- hot retry loop, which is what RetryAfterBackoff exists to prevent.

    @guarantee AcceptanceCarriesTheIdentity
        -- Acceptance says who the store believes this client is. The same
        -- client presenting the same credential is the same identity, every
        -- time, across machines and across restarts — that stability is what
        -- host.allium: AdoptUserIdentity relies on, and it is the store's to
        -- provide rather than the board's to reconstruct.

    @guarantee EveryMutationIsAtomicAndAnswered
        -- A mutation sent to the store ends in exactly one of acceptance or
        -- rejection, and either way the store's rows are left in a state that
        -- some single mutation could have produced. Partly applied is not an
        -- outcome. A store that could leave half a mutation standing would make
        -- StoreRejectsAnInvalidMutation's all-or-nothing claim false, and there
        -- is no query a client could run afterwards to find out which half.
        --
        -- ANSWERED, not merely performed. Several mutations return something
        -- the caller needs in order to continue — the id of a task it is about
        -- to dispatch, whether a claim was won, what a cleared session was
        -- holding. A store that applied a write and said nothing would leave
        -- those callers guessing, and the guess is a race.
        --
        -- The rejection carries a reason in terms the operator can act on, for
        -- the same reason EveryFailureNamesItself demands one of the indicator.

    @guarantee TheStoreEnforcesTheSharedRules
        -- Validation and every derived shared value are the store's, computed
        -- over all the rows rather than over one board's subscription. The
        -- worked example is an epic's status: its children can belong to
        -- different people on different machines, no single board subscribes to
        -- all of them, and two boards each deriving from what they can see
        -- would write two different answers and keep overwriting each other.
        -- See epics.allium: EpicStatusRecalculation.

    @guarantee SendsOnlyWhatWasSubscribedTo
        -- Rows reach this board only for its subscriptions: the connected
        -- identity's own user board, and the epics in `subscribed_epics`.
        -- SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics constrains what is
        -- ASKED for; this is the counterpart obligation on what is SENT, and
        -- it is the half a client cannot enforce for itself.

    @guidance
        -- Nothing here names a transport. Sockets, polling and a person
        -- carrying a disk all satisfy this surface; what does not is anything
        -- that cannot report a drop.
}

surface ConnectionIndicator {
    facing operator: Operator

    context connection: BoardConnection

    exposes:
        connection.status
        connection.server
        connection.last_error when connection.status = disconnected | failed
        connection.attempts when connection.status = disconnected

    @guarantee DisconnectedIsAlwaysVisible
        -- A board that is not connected says so, continuously, for as long as
        -- that is true. Not a message that appears once and scrolls away: the
        -- danger is a person trusting a board that stopped updating an hour ago,
        -- and a notification they already dismissed does not protect them.

    @guarantee ConnectedIsQuiet
        -- The healthy state is the overwhelmingly common one, and an indicator
        -- that draws attention to it teaches people to ignore the indicator.

    @guarantee EveryFailureNamesItself
        -- `disconnected` and `failed` both carry a reason an operator can act
        -- on — which host, refused or timed out, which two identities. "Sync
        -- error" is not an acceptable message for the only thing on screen
        -- telling somebody why their colleague's work is missing.

    @guidance
        -- `attempts` is exposed while disconnected because it is the difference
        -- between "just now" and "for the last twenty minutes", and those call
        -- for different reactions from the person reading it. It is not exposed
        -- in `failed`: retrying is not happening and a count would imply it is.
}

surface EpicSubscriptionToggle {
    facing operator: Operator

    context epic: core/Epic

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    exposes:
        epic in connection.subscribed_epics

    provides:
        SubscribeToEpic(operator, epic)
            when not (epic in connection.subscribed_epics)
        UnsubscribeFromEpic(operator, epic)
            when epic in connection.subscribed_epics

    @guarantee SubscriptionStateIsVisibleBeforeItIsChanged
        -- Whether this board follows an epic is readable from the epic itself.
        -- A toggle whose current position is invisible is a button that does
        -- one of two opposite things.

    @guidance
        -- Deliberately attached to the epic rather than gathered into a
        -- subscription manager somewhere. The moment a person decides they care
        -- about an epic is the moment they are looking at it, and a separate
        -- screen is a screen they have to know exists.
        --
        -- The two actions are guarded into a toggle rather than offered as a
        -- pair, so that the operation available is always the one that changes
        -- something.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- ANSWERED. It is refused, loudly, and nothing changes anywhere — see
-- AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused and NoWriteIsEverQueued. Queueing lost on the
-- argument the question itself set up: a replayed write carries an intent
-- formed against a board state that no longer holds, and the operator was told
-- it had already worked. The refusal reaches somebody who is still looking at
-- the screen.

open question "What is a board supposed to do with a change whose fate is unknown? EveryMutationIsAtomicAndAnswered admits acceptance and rejection, and a transport has a third outcome: the change was SENT and then the connection went, or the store never answered within config.mutation_timeout. It may or may not have been applied, and nothing here says who is told what. Re-sending is the one answer that is definitely wrong — it is the queue NoWriteIsEverQueued refuses, with the added hazard that the original may have landed."

open question "Should a change have to wait for a SETTLED identity, not merely a connected transport? NothingIsSubscribedBeforeAnIdentityExists says nothing is ASKED FOR before the identity is settled, and there is no counterpart for what is WRITTEN. The window is real: acceptance comes before the verdict, so a change made in between is made by somebody this install may turn out not to be. Two of them already refuse on their own account, because they need a name to stamp; the rest do not."

open question "Should the board expose a way to retry NOW, rather than waiting out the backoff? An operator who has just fixed the store knows something the backoff does not. Left out for the moment because config.reconnect_backoff_max is a minute, which is short enough that the button might only exist to be pressed impatiently."

-- ANSWERED. The board reads from the subscription now, and the round trip from
-- process start to a complete subscription is 4-44 ms on loopback against an
-- empty database, held under a ceiling by a test against a live instance. The
-- cold start is still not BLOCKED on it — OpenBoardConnection fires after the
-- board is drawn — so what the number really bounds is how long a configured
-- board shows nothing before it shows something.

open question "Is a periodic refresh still worth keeping now that rows arrive unasked? It is a backstop against a delivery that never comes — a callback the SDK drops, a subscription that silently stops — and it costs nothing when nothing has changed. But a backstop nobody can observe failing is also a backstop nobody knows has failed, and the board would be quietly polling its way through a broken subscription rather than reporting one."

open question "What does a board with no store configured read once the local shared tables are gone? Today it reads them, which is what keeps the single-machine install first-class. The migration's last phase drops those tables, and at that point either the single-machine install stops existing or it needs a store of its own — a local one, an embedded one, or a re-admitted SQLite. Nothing here chooses, and the choice should not be made by the phase that happens to delete the tables."

open question "Is `failed` recoverable without restarting the board? It is terminal in the graph, so today an identity conflict is resolved by fixing the credential and starting again. Whether a resolved conflict should be able to re-open the connection in place is undecided, and depends on a remedy flow nobody has designed."
